ce><volume>22</volume>, <fpage>337</fpage>&#x02013;<lpage>345</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1016/S0166-2236(99)01407-1</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10407416</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B28"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Tallon-Baudry</surname><given-names>C.</given-names></name><name><surname>Kreiter</surname><given-names>A.</given-names></name><name><surname>Bertrand</surname><given-names>O.</given-names></name></person-group> (<year>1999</year>). <article-title>Sustained and transient oscillatory responses in the gamma and beta bands in a visual short-term memory task in humans</article-title>. <source>Vis. Neurosci.</source><volume>16</volume>, <fpage>449</fpage>&#x02013;<lpage>459</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1017/S0952523899163065</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10349966</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B29"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Thomson</surname><given-names>A. M.</given-names></name><name><surname>Bannister</surname><given-names>A. P.</given-names></name><name><surname>Mercer</surname><given-names>A.</given-names></name><name><surname>Morris</surname><given-names>O. T.</given-names></name></person-group> (<year>2002</year>). <article-title>Target and temporal pattern selection at neocortical synapses</article-title>. <source>Philos. Trans. R. Soc. Lond., B, Biol. Sci.</source><volume>357</volume>, <fpage>1781</fpage>&#x02013;<lpage>1791</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1098/rstb.2002.1163</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12626012</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B30"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Thomson</surname><given-names>A. M.</given-names></name><name><surname>Morris</surname><given-names>O. T.</given-names></name></person-group> (<year>2002</year>). <article-title>Selectivity in the inter-laminar connections made by neocortical neurones</article-title>. <source>J. Neurocytol.</source><volume>31</volume>, <fpage>239</fpage>&#x02013;<lpage>246</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1023/A:1024117908539</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12815243</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B31"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Traub</surname><given-names>R. D.</given-names></name><name><surname>Cunningham</surname><given-names>M. O.</given-names></name><name><surname>Gloveli</surname><given-names>T.</given-names></name><name><surname>LeBeau</surname><given-names>F. E.</given-names></name><name><surname>Bibbig</surname><given-names>A.</given-names></name></person-group> (<year>2003</year>). <article-title>GABA-enhanced collective behavior in neuronal axons underlies persistent gamma-frequency oscillations</article-title>. <source>Proc. Natl. Acad. Sci. USA</source><volume>100</volume>, <fpage>11047</fpage>&#x02013;<lpage>11052</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1073/pnas.1934854100</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12960382</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B32"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Traub</surname><given-names>R. D.</given-names></name><name><surname>Whittington</surname><given-names>M. A.</given-names></name><name><surname>Stanford</surname><given-names>I. M.</given-names></name><name><surname>Jefferys</surname><given-names>J. G.</given-names></name></person-group> (<year>1996</year>). <article-title>A mechanism for generation of long-range synchronous fast oscillations in the cortex</article-title>. <source>Nature</source><volume>383</volume>, <fpage>621</fpage>&#x02013;<lpage>624</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1038/383621a0</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8857537</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B33"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Vidal</surname><given-names>J. R.</given-names></name><name><surname>Chaumon</surname><given-names>M.</given-names></name><name><surname>O'Regan</surname><given-names>J. K.</given-names></name><name><surname>Tallon-Baudry</surname><given-names>C.</given-names></name></person-group> (<year>2006</year>). <article-title>Visual grouping and the focusing of attention induce gamma-band oscillations at different frequencies in human magnetoencephalogram signals</article-title>. <source>J. Cogn. Neurosci.</source><volume>18</volume>, <fpage>1850</fpage>&#x02013;<lpage>1862</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1162/jocn.2006.18.11.1850</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">17069476</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B34"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Weiss</surname><given-names>H.</given-names></name><name><surname>Weiss</surname><given-names>V.</given-names></name></person-group> (<year>2003</year>). <article-title>The golden mean as clock cycle of brainwaves</article-title>. <source>Chaos Solitons Fractals</source><volume>4</volume>, <fpage>643</fpage>&#x02013;<lpage>652</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1016/S0960-0779(03)00026-2</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B35"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Whittington</surname><given-names>M. A.</given-names></name><name><surname>Traub</surname><given-names>R. D.</given-names></name><name><surname>Faulkner</surname><given-names>H. J.</given-names></name><name><surname>Stanford</surname><given-names>I. M.</given-names></name><name><surname>Jefferys</surname><given-names>J. G.</given-names></name></person-group> (<year>1997</year>). <article-title>Recurrent excitatory postsynaptic potentials induced by synchronized fast cortical oscillations</article-title>. <source>Proc. Natl. Acad. Sci. USA</source><volume>94</volume>, <fpage>12198</fpage>&#x02013;<lpage>12203</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1073/pnas.94.22.12198</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9342386</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B36"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Wiskott</surname><given-names>L.</given-names></name><name><surname>Sejnowski</surname><given-names>T. J.</given-names></name></person-group> (<year>2002</year>) <article-title>Slow feature analysis: unsupervised learning of invariances</article-title>. <source>Neural Comput.</source><volume>14</volume>, <fpage>715</fpage>&#x02013;<lpage>770</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1162/089976602317318938</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11936959</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B37"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Womelsdorf</surname><given-names>T.</given-names></name><name><surname>Fries</surname><given-names>P.</given-names></name><name><surname>Mitra</surname><given-names>P. P.</given-names></name><name><surname>Desimone</surname><given-names>R.</given-names></name></person-group> (<year>2006</year>). <article-title>Gamma-band synchronization in visual cortex predicts speed of change detection</article-title>. <source>Nature</source><volume>439</volume>, <fpage>733</fpage>&#x02013;<lpage>736</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1038/nature04258</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16372022</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B38"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Ylinen</surname><given-names>A.</given-names></name><name><surname>Bragin</surname><given-names>A.</given-names></name><name><surname>N&#x000e1;dasdy</surname><given-names>Z.</given-names></name><name><surname>Jand&#x000f3;</surname><given-names>G.</given-names></name><name><surname>Szab&#x000f3;</surname><given-names>I.</given-names></name><etal/></person-group> (<year>1995</year>). <article-title>Sharp wave-associated high-frequency oscillation (200 Hz) in the intact hippocampus: network and intracellular mechanisms</article-title>. <source>J. Neurosci.</source><volume>15</volume>, <fpage>30</fpage>&#x02013;<lpage>46</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">7823136</pub-id></citation></ref></ref-list><glossary><title>Key Concept</title><def-list><def-item><term id="G1">Population rhythms</term><def><p>Periodic electrical activity changes resulting from the intrinsic properties of, and interactions between, many thousands of neurons in the brain.</p></def></def-item><def-item><term id="G2">Spectral processing</term><def><p>The idea that different features of a sensory object are coded by different frequencies of population rhythm.</p></def></def-item><def-item><term id="G3">Period concatenation</term><def><p>A combination of <xref ref-type="other" rid="G4">frequency interaction</xref> and <xref ref-type="other" rid="G5">frequency transition</xref> whereby changes in connectivity <italic>between</italic> two oscillating networks results in sequential activation of each network on a period-by-period basis. The resulting frequency is derived from the sum of the period durations of the original two oscillations.</p></def></def-item><def-item><term id="G4">Frequency interactions</term><def><p>The manner in which rhythmic neuronal activity at one frequency may influence co-expressed activity at another frequency &#x02013; usually quantified in terms of relative amplitude and phase relationships.</p></def></def-item><def-item><term id="G5">Frequency transitions</term><def><p>The phenomenon whereby one population rhythm may abruptly alter modal frequency on the basis of change in synaptic weights within - and neuromodulatory input to &#x02013; a neuronal network.</p></def></def-item></def-list></glossary><bio><p><inline-graphic xlink:href="fnins-02-145-i001.gif"/><bold>Miles Whittington's</bold> laboratory in the Institute of Neuroscience at Newcastle University investigates the neuronal network mechanisms underlying rhythmic population behaviour associated with electroencephalographic signals in health and disease. Communication between neurons often occurs hand-in-hand with a range of discrete oscillatory phenomena. These phenomena correlate with many behavioural, cognitive and motor states in the whole organism. We aim to understand the mechanisms underlying these oscillations to aid identification of pathology underlying neurological and psychiatric illnesses.</p></bio></back></article>